## 2026-09-26 — SRS Revision 175: temporary Production at bodouralamal.com, sign-in not offered
- `GET /site-config` → `{ sign_in_offered }` (public, anonymous, no cookie, no personal data, `no-store`), from `SIGN_IN_OFFERED`; **absent = offered**, so every other tier is unchanged. The shared `SignInButton` renders only when offered, which hides it in the header, the mobile menu and the landing hero at once; `/login`, `/register` and the OAuth flow are untouched by Owner decision.
- Guard `check-signin-offered.sh` (proven by adding a raw OAuth link to the header → red): one control, and it asks the deployment; `/login` and `/register` are the stated exceptions. `robots.txt` allows the site, declines to advertise `/login` and `/register`.
- Verified for the privacy notice: access log carries time/request-id/method/status/bytes/timings only — **no IP, no URL, no user-agent**; the API never reads the address; `AuditLog` has no address column; a reader receives no cookie; CSP `default-src 'self'` with self-hosted fonts. **The approved IP anonymisation was unnecessary — nothing to anonymise.**
- Verification: backend typecheck/lint, OpenAPI regenerated (192 paths / 248 ops) + TD-3 conformance, frontend tsc/lint/1514 tests (5 new), doc links, `git diff --check`.
- R175 §4 host work: one certificate for `bodouralamal.com` + `www` + `staging` (issued, expires 2026-12-25); `enable-tls.sh` takes further server names for the same certificate; **the memory ceilings moved into `docker-compose.production.yml`** — they had lived only in the staging overlay, and a tier switch would have dropped them on a 4 GB host.
- CI 6/8 on `2051ef50`: the pending-denial guard enumerates every TD-3 route and expects refusal — `/site-config` had to be declared public there beside `/clock`, with its reason (it describes the deployment, never the caller). 239/239 on the disposable stack.
- R175 §4 — the four-CPU Production floor is about RECORDING, so the preflight now asks (`BODOUR_TIER_RECORDS=no`, printed loudly, absent = records) instead of assuming; `MIN_CPUS_PRODUCTION=4` stands for every tier that records. B8 repository, restic password and `/etc/bodour-recovery.env` created on the host (password never printed; the Owner escrows it).
- Found on the live site: the flag's fallback said «a failure means offered», so the control flashed onto the public page for the seconds an API restart takes. Reversed — an unanswered question offers nothing (and when the API is unreachable, sign-in could not have worked anyway). Pinned in a test.
- `check-signin-offered.sh` flagged the adapter's own explanation: it matched the bare path, so prose naming `/api/v1/auth/google` read as a link. It now matches a quoted literal (re-proven: a raw `href` in the header still fails the build). `session-recording-ingest`'s worker-restart test failed once on the runner and passes 41/41 locally — a timing flake, watched.
- **Deployed to Production at `bodouralamal.com` (`21381350`)**: preflight PASS with the no-recording declaration, TLS active on the three names, `/healthz` 200, zero error lines. Verified in a real browser: `/`, `/calendar`, `/resources` offer **0** sign-in links; `/login` and `/register` keep theirs (1 each); every page `https:`, **0** insecure resources, **0** cookies for a reader.
- Measurement defect found in my own tooling: the scratchpad screenshot helper hard-coded `http://localhost` and ignored `APP_BASE`, so three rounds of "live" screenshots were of Localhost (where the button correctly shows). Fixed; the live shots then matched the probes.
- R175 §5 — the previous site (Firebase `seeds-of-hope-51cb7`) still rendered in the Owner's browser at `/api/v1/auth/google` while `/` rendered ours. Server proven right throughout (DNS from 3 public resolvers + authoritative; `curl` 302 → Google on the path, its trailing slash and the callback): the copy was in the browser's own cache, for an address the old site answered as a document and this one does not. **Not DNS, and not the OAuth client** — the client id chooses which Google project authenticates, not which page a cache returns; the working client stays.
- Fix: `/clear-cache` answers `Clear-Site-Data: "cache", "storage"` — one visit discards the origin's HTTP cache, CacheStorage, `localStorage` and any service worker; cookies deliberately kept, so nobody is signed out. On its own URL, not the shell, or every visit would re-download the bundle (§2.2). Both OAuth redirects now send `no-store` (one-time `state`; a 302 is not heuristically cacheable, so this states the rule instead of relying on the default).
- Found by testing, not reading: Nginx types a response from the **request's** extension, so the aliased `.html` on an extensionless URI went out `application/octet-stream` and `nosniff` turned the recovery page into a download. `default_type text/html` + `charset utf-8`. Also: `nginx -s reload` in the dev container reported success without applying the change — a restart was needed to see it.
- Verification: `/clear-cache` on the dev stack returns 200 `text/html; charset=utf-8` with `Clear-Site-Data`, `no-store`, CSP, nosniff and HSTS; 3 unit tests (both redirects, entry + callback) and 1 HTTP integration assertion on the wire; 31 guards, backend typecheck/lint, doc links. Runbook: «A visitor still sees the previous website».
- Same CSP reasoning found a second defect: `style-src 'self'` forbids an inline `<style>`, and the TD-16 maintenance page carried one — it has rendered unstyled wherever it rendered, silently. Both recovery pages now link `/recovery.css` (a plain file in the build, not a hashed asset: these pages serve when the app cannot). Verified in a real browser at 390 px, document width 390, no overflow.
- Open, reported not changed: that interstitial cannot fire — `error_page 502 503 504` sits in `location /`, which has no upstream. A forced outage returns the TD-3.8 envelope from `@api_unavailable`, which the client's §14.4 offline state already renders, so the SRS behaviour is delivered by the app.
- My own defect, caught by CI not by me: `auth-session-serialization.integration.test.ts` builds its own `res` stub and I had only completed the other one, so `res.set` threw **inside the transaction under test** and five rollback assertions reported «got res.set is not a function» instead of the error they were provoking. Stub completed; 18/18 on the disposable stack, the new `no-store` wire assertion included.
- R175 §6 — Owner decision: the Google OAuth client stays in the project that issued it. Investigated first: `sub` is per-project, so a swap is an account migration, not config. Measured on the live tier — 6 users, 6 active identities, 1 with `pre_provisioned_email`; the other five would meet the registration form as strangers and the sixth would be locked out by `user_identity_provider_email_active_key`. The consequence (**do not delete that project**) is recorded in `operations/configuration.md`, where someone tidying up will actually read it.
